# CXO Hive \'97 Content Intelligence\
\
## PURPOSE\
\
Build a long-term daily content engine that discovers what is most likely to attract meaningful engagement from CXO Hive's ICP.\
\
The objective is not simply to find topics.\
\
The objective is to discover:\
\
- What content is already working\
- Why it is working\
- What the ICP is paying attention to\
- What the ICP is discussing or feeling\
- What is changing in the market\
- What is newly relevant\
- What competitors and adjacent creators are doing successfully\
- What content patterns can be adapted\
- What CXO Hive can contribute credibly\
- What opportunities are worth turning into posts\
\
The ultimate business objective is qualified ICP engagement:\
\
ICP attention \uc0\u8594  engagement \u8594  profile interest \u8594  credibility \u8594  conversation \u8594  consult \u8594  paid coaching.\
\
Content should therefore optimize for both:\
1. Attention and engagement\
2. Relevance and quality of the audience engaging\
\
Do not optimize for vanity metrics alone.\
\
---\
\
## SOURCE-FIRST, THEN HOP\
\
Do not start research by inventing keyword queries or generating topics.\
\
Start from the standing source map (named news outlets, seed ICP operators, seed adjacent creators). Dig those sources first.\
\
Then hop:\
\
- News: read the named outlets first. Then search LinkedIn for whether the ICP is actually reacting.\
- LinkedIn: scrape seed operators and seed adjacent creators first. Keyword search is a fallback, not the opening move.\
- When a useful phrase, tension, or account appears, follow it to the next source. The strongest opportunities are often two hops from the seed: a post \uc0\u8594  a commenter \u8594  that person's thread.\
\
Unexpected topics are still allowed. Discover them from a source, not instead of a source.\
\
A large creator audience, high comment count, or a viral post is not automatically ICP-relevant.\
\
The living list of outlets, accounts, and phrases belongs in the source map. Do not turn this file into a URL list.\
\
---\
\
## WHAT THE RESEARCH SHOULD COVER\
\
Research should continuously explore multiple sources of content opportunity.\
\
### 1. PROVEN LINKEDIN CONTENT\
\
Find high-performing LinkedIn posts from people whose audiences overlap with CXO Hive's ICP.\
\
Start with seed operators and seed creators from the source map. Use keyword search only after that, or when a newly discovered phrase needs a wider check.\
\
Research both:\
\
- India\
- Global\
\
Relevant creator/author categories may include:\
\
- Executive coaches\
- Leadership coaches\
- Career coaches\
- Executive presence experts\
- Leadership thinkers\
- Senior technology leaders\
- CXOs\
- CEOs\
- GCC leaders\
- Technology leadership voices\
- Career/promotion voices\
- Other creators with substantial ICP audience overlap\
\
Do not treat CXO Hive's own account, or a single lookalike coach, as the market. Own-account performance is calibration unless qualified ICP people are clearly in the engagement.\
\
The goal is to understand what is actually working in the market.\
\
For every strong LinkedIn source, preserve where available:\
\
- Author\
- Post URL\
- Full post text\
- Date\
- Reactions\
- Comments\
- Reposts\
- Impressions/reach if available\
- Engagement information\
- Relevant comment quality\
- Evidence of ICP engagement\
- Topic\
- Format\
- Hook\
- Structure\
- Underlying idea\
- Why the post appears to have worked\
\
Never invent unavailable metrics.\
\
---\
\
### 2. ICP CONTENT BEHAVIOR\
\
Research what the actual ICP is engaging with.\
\
Look for:\
\
- What they react to\
- What they comment on\
- What discussions they participate in\
- What topics repeatedly appear in their conversations\
- What problems they describe\
- What opinions generate disagreement\
- What stories cause people to share their own experience\
\
ICP engagement is particularly valuable because a large creator audience does not automatically mean the creator's content is relevant to CXO Hive's audience.\
\
Prefer posts and threads written or engaged with by ICP operators over posts that only perform with coaches, recruiters, or personal-brand accounts.\
\
Where possible, distinguish:\
\
generic engagement\
\
from\
\
qualified ICP engagement.\
\
---\
\
### 3. COMPETITOR AND ADJACENT CREATOR INTELLIGENCE\
\
Study companies, creators and experts operating in adjacent or overlapping spaces.\
\
Start from the seed creator list in the source map, then expand when a new creator repeatedly reaches the ICP.\
\
The purpose is not only competitor monitoring.\
\
Look for:\
\
- Winning topics\
- Winning hooks\
- Successful structures\
- Formats\
- Story patterns\
- Contrarian ideas\
- Questions\
- Emotional triggers\
- Recurring themes\
- Content gaps\
- Audience reactions\
- What appears saturated\
- What appears underdeveloped\
\
Use successful content as inspiration and intelligence.\
\
Do not simply copy it.\
\
Extract the underlying idea and determine how CXO Hive could develop an original version.\
\
---\
\
### 4. CURRENT NEWS AND MARKET DEVELOPMENTS\
\
Research developments from approximately:\
\
- Last 24\'9672 hours\
- Last 7 days\
\
depending on the subject.\
\
Start with the named news outlets in the source map. Then check whether the ICP is discussing the development on LinkedIn.\
\
Look for developments that may matter to the ICP, including:\
\
- AI announcements\
- AI changing technology roles\
- Layoffs\
- GCC expansion or contraction\
- Executive appointments\
- Major technology changes\
- Indian IT developments\
- Organizational changes\
- Leadership changes\
- Regulation\
- Economic developments\
- Enterprise technology changes\
- Workforce changes\
- Major business announcements\
- New research\
- Major public conversations\
\
Do not assume every news event is a content opportunity.\
\
A news event without evidence that the ICP cares is a weak opportunity, even if the story is large.\
\
Determine whether it creates a meaningful question, tension, insight or discussion for the ICP.\
\
---\
\
### 5. EMERGING TRENDS\
\
Look for topics that are becoming relevant before they become completely saturated.\
\
The agent should identify:\
\
- Emerging conversations\
- New terminology\
- New career pressures\
- Changing expectations of senior leaders\
- New technology-driven leadership challenges\
- New organizational patterns\
- New GCC dynamics\
- New expectations around executive leadership\
- Unexpected topics gaining attention\
\
The agent should be allowed to discover opportunities outside the predefined CXO Hive pillars.\
\
The ICP is the primary audience constraint.\
\
The topic itself does not have to fit one predefined pillar.\
\
---\
\
### 6. CXO HIVE-SPECIFIC OPPORTUNITIES\
\
Some opportunities may come directly from CXO Hive's own knowledge and experience rather than external trends.\
\
Look for opportunities where:\
\
- CXO Hive has genuine expertise\
- Existing CXO Hive evidence supports the idea\
- Existing customer language reveals an important insight\
- A known customer problem is poorly discussed publicly\
- CXO Hive has a differentiated perspective\
- An existing concept can be expressed in a more interesting way\
\
These opportunities do not need to be forced into sponsorship, positioning, presence or evidence.\
\
Those are important areas of expertise, not mandatory content subjects.\
\
CXO Hive's own posts can reveal which ideas already attract attention. They are not proof the ICP is engaging unless the engagers are qualified ICP.\
\
---\
\
## CONTENT SHOULD BE INTERESTING FIRST\
\
Content should have a reason for someone to stop scrolling.\
\
Potential characteristics include:\
\
- Strong curiosity\
- A surprising observation\
- A useful insight\
- A contradiction\
- A strong opinion\
- A recognizable experience\
- A timely development\
- A counterintuitive idea\
- A story\
- A question\
- A new interpretation of something happening in the market\
\
Do not turn every finding into educational "5 tips" content.\
\
Do not force every opportunity to directly sell coaching.\
\
---\
\
## THE 60 / 20 / 10 / 10 CONTENT MIX\
\
Use this as a strategic target for the eventual content system:\
\
### ~60% Proven\
\
Ideas, themes, formats or patterns already showing strong evidence of working.\
\
### ~20% Current / Trending\
\
New developments, current conversations, news and timely opportunities.\
\
### ~10% CXO Hive-specific expertise\
\
Strong ideas supported primarily by CXO Hive's own knowledge, experience or evidence.\
\
### ~10% Experimental\
\
Unusual angles, new formats, contrarian ideas or ideas with less historical evidence but potentially high upside.\
\
This is a target distribution, not a rigid mathematical requirement.\
\
Do not select a weak opportunity merely to satisfy the percentage.\
\
---\
\
