\
The research system must always retain the origin of an opportunity.\
\
---\
\
## CONTENT OPPORTUNITY\
\
A content opportunity is more than a topic.\
\
For example:\
\
WEAK:\
"Executive presence"\
\
STRONGER:\
"Senior technology leaders can be technically credible and still fail to land as the person leadership wants to put in charge."\
\
The opportunity should capture the underlying idea, tension, observation or conversation.\
\
The eventual content writer can then turn the opportunity into the actual post.\
\
---\
\
## ICP IS THE AUDIENCE FILTER, NOT A CONTENT CAGE\
\
Content should generally remain relevant to the ICP.\
\
However, do not force every post to explicitly discuss:\
\
- Sponsorship\
- Positioning\
- Promotion\
- Executive presence\
- Career advancement\
\
The agent should be able to identify broader topics when there is a strong reason to believe the ICP will care.\
\
For example, an AI development may be relevant because it changes what senior technology leaders are expected to do.\
\
The agent should ask:\
\
"Why would our ICP care?"\
\
rather than:\
\
"Can I force this into sponsorship?"\
\
---\
\
## WHAT SHOULD ULTIMATELY BE OPTIMIZED\
\
Primary:\
\
Qualified ICP engagement.\
\
Secondary:\
\
- Overall engagement\
- Reach\
- Visibility\
- Conversation potential\
- Profile interest\
- Authority\
\
Commercial relevance matters, but content should not become direct-response advertising.\
\
A post can be valuable because it attracts the right audience and strengthens authority even if nobody books a consult directly from that post.\
\
---\
\
## CORE PRINCIPLE\
\
The system should continuously answer:\
\
"What is working?"\
\
"Who is engaging with it?"\
\
"Why is it working?"\
\
"What is changing?"\
\
"What does our ICP care about?"\
\
"What is missing?"\
\
"What could CXO Hive do with this?"\
\
Then convert those findings into a ranked set of content opportunities.}